## Evalkit groups agent traces to build evaluation cases

Source: Evalkit — https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50655285
Published: 2026-11-26
Channel: hn

Evalkit is an open-source framework for improving AI agents through evaluations, or repeatable checks of their behavior. Its creators say teams can record traces of agent runs in existing tools, but often have to assemble their own process for turning failures into tests and tracking regressions.

The first-party announcement describes a workflow that starts by mining traces and grouping similar ones into issue buckets. An expert can review the resulting cases using plain-English rubrics, rather than writing a structured JSON rubric. The cases can then be rerun as datasets to establish baseline scores or internal benchmarks. The creators say they continue to use self-hosted Phoenix as a trace database and designed Evalkit so a coding agent can perform much of the workflow. They describe one internal use in which an issue bucket led to suggested cases and merged changes, along with reductions in failures, token use and time. Those figures are the creators' account of one use, not an independent comparison. The project is early, and its creators say they are still adding features.

Why it matters: Teams that already collect agent traces can examine whether grouping and expert-reviewed cases fit their evaluation process.

